## 9. Our System — GEO-RESQ, What It Can Do

**Full name:** GEO-RESQ — Post-Disaster Intelligence & Emergency Decision-Support Platform ("From Earth Observation to Actionable Rescue Intelligence")

**Core capability, in one line:** fuses pre/post-disaster satellite imagery, upstream IoT water-level telemetry, and open GIS/mapping data into one continuously updated, routable operational picture — showing responders what changed, what's likely to happen next, and where to act.

### What it does today vs. what's proposed (stated honestly, per the document's own Table 2)

| Capability | Status |
|---|---|
| AI damage-detection architecture (Bi-Temporal Siamese U-Net) | Prototype — architecture defined, validation pending |
| IoT water-level sensing | Prototype hardware — bench/field-tested |
| Surge/trend engine (rate of rise, acceleration → risk & ETA) | Prototype logic — basic rate-of-rise detection implemented |
| Command dashboard | Prototype UI — demonstration build, mock data |
| Satellite EO ingestion (live) | Proposed — not yet integrated |
| Automated vectorization (raster → GIS-usable roads/buildings/bridges) | Proposed |
| Damage-aware routing | Proposed — no routing engine integrated yet |
| OpenStreetMap/HOT auto-submission | Proposed — no automatic contribution implemented |

### The four-stage pipeline
1. **Disaster & Multi-Modal Inputs** — pre/post satellite EO, IoT water-level telemetry, existing GIS/OSM data, field/crowdsourced reports
2. **AI Processing Core** — Siamese U-Net change detection, automated vectorization, damage classification (tagged `destroyed:highway`, `blocked:road`, `damaged:bridge`, `washed_out:road` in OSM-compatible convention)
3. **Real-Time Surge Engine** — water-level trend analysis producing risk + estimated-time-of-arrival signals downstream
4. **Rescue Dispatch & Interoperability** — command dashboard output, publishable to OSM/HOT and government GIS where integrated

### Target users
Government disaster-management agencies (NADMA-type bodies), emergency services (fire/rescue, police, medical), humanitarian organizations (Red Cross/Crescent, HOT), infrastructure operators, and — longer-term — catastrophe insurers. Initial go-to-market: Malaysian government/emergency-service licensing, expanding to ASEAN/APAC.

### Market framing
TAM anchored to the named, cited geospatial-imagery-analytics market (disaster-management segment): USD 12.12B (2025) → USD 18.55B (2030). Bottom-up SOM: ~30–50 addressable Malaysian government/agency licences over Years 1–3, at an illustrative USD 15,000–40,000/licence, ≈ USD 0.5M–2M/yr modelled (not forecast) opportunity.

### Responsible-AI stance
Explicitly positioned as decision support, never autonomous command — every AI output (damage classification, surge alert) is designed for human review before it drives dispatch action, carries a confidence score, and is traceable to its source imagery/sensor reading.

### The gap that matters most right now
Section 10 of the GEO-RESQ document lists its AI/cloud stack as PyTorch/TensorFlow, FastAPI, PostgreSQL — with **"Huawei Cloud, ModelArts, Ascend AI" marked as Proposed, not implemented.** Compared against Section 8's mandatory-Huawei-technology rule and HyperNeura's fully Huawei-native pipeline (OBS → MindSpore → ModelArts → ECS → Speech Kit), this is the single highest-priority item to close before submission — see the evaluation and options already discussed for how to approach it within the 27 September deadline.

